\title{A Log-Concavity Dichotomy for the Total $d$-Hoggatt Numbers}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-07}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The \emph{total $d$-Hoggatt numbers} are the row sums $H_d(n) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} H_d(n,k)$ of the $d$-Hoggatt triangle. For the smallest parameters they specialize to fundamental combinatorial sequences: $H_1(n) = 2^n$ (the row sums of Pascal's triangle), $H_2(n) = C_n$ (the Catalan numbers), and $H_3(n)$ (the Baxter numbers). We study the \emph{infinite log-concavity} of these totals---the property that the log-concavity operator $\Lop$, defined by $(\Lop a)(n) = a_{n+1}^2 - a_n a_{n+2}$, produces a nonnegative sequence under arbitrarily many iterations. We prove a sharp dichotomy between the two smallest rungs of the hierarchy: the $d=1$ totals $2^n$ are infinitely log-concave (indeed \emph{log-linear}, annihilated by $\Lop$), while the $d=2$ totals---the Catalan numbers---are \emph{strictly log-convex}, failing log-concavity at every index. The Catalan result rests on a single exact polynomial identity, $(2n+3)(n+2) - (2n+1)(n+3) = 3$, whose stubborn positive residue forces the discriminant negative for all $n$. These results overturn the natural conjecture that the totals inherit log-concavity from the individual rows and reframe the problem: for $d \ge 2$ the totals appear to be universally log-convex, a ``summation amplification'' phenomenon. We discuss the extension to Baxter numbers, the conjectural universal log-convexity of the totals, and a renormalization program under which infinite log-concavity may be recovered.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} log-concavity, log-convexity, infinite log-concavity, Hoggatt triangle, Catalan numbers, Baxter numbers, binomial coefficients, unimodality.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
Log-concavity is one of the organizing principles of enumerative combinatorics. A sequence $(a_n)_{n\ge0}$ of positive reals is \emph{log-concave} if $a_{n+1}^2 \ge a_n a_{n+2}$ for all $n$, equivalently if its consecutive ratios $a_{n+1}/a_n$ are nonincreasing. Log-concave sequences are unimodal, they arise as coefficient sequences of real-rooted polynomials, and their ubiquity---from the binomial coefficients to the coefficients of chromatic and characteristic polynomials---has made log-concavity conjectures a recurring theme, several of which were resolved only recently through substantial machinery.

A far more delicate notion is \emph{infinite log-concavity}. Introduced through the \emph{log-concavity operator}
\[
(\Lop a)(n) = a_{n+1}^2 - a_n a_{n+2},
\]
a sequence is infinitely log-concave if $\Lop^k a \ge 0$ (pointwise) for every $k \ge 0$. Because $\Lop a$ is again a sequence, this is a demanding closure requirement across a bottomless hierarchy of derived sequences. The infinite log-concavity of the binomial coefficients $\binom{n}{k}$ (rows of Pascal's triangle) is a well-known hard problem, resolved through careful analysis of the operator's action.

\subsection{The $d$-Hoggatt hierarchy}
The $d$-Hoggatt triangles $H_d(n,k)$ form a one-parameter family generalizing Pascal's triangle. Their \emph{totals} (row sums)
\[
H_d(n) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} H_d(n,k)
\]
interpolate across some of the most studied sequences in combinatorics:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccl}
\toprule
$d$ & Total sequence $H_d(n)$ & First terms \\
\midrule
$1$ & Powers of two $2^n$ & $1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, \dots$ \\
$2$ & Catalan numbers $C_n$ & $1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42, 132, \dots$ \\
$3$ & Baxter numbers $B_n$ & $1, 1, 2, 6, 22, 92, 422, \dots$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The single parameter $d$ tunes the totals from the arithmetically trivial ($2^n$) to sequences whose finer analytic properties remain the subject of active research.

\subsection{Contributions}
We settle the question at the two smallest rungs and expose the mechanism governing the rest.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{(Log-linear totals, $d=1$).} Every geometric sequence $a_n = r^n$ satisfies $\Lop a = 0$ identically, hence is infinitely log-concave. In particular $H_1(n) = 2^n$ is infinitely log-concave.
\item \textbf{(Catalan log-convexity, $d=2$).} The Catalan numbers are \emph{strictly log-convex}: $C_{n+1}^2 < C_n C_{n+2}$ for all $n$. Hence they are not log-concave, and a fortiori not infinitely log-concave. The proof reduces to the exact identity $(2n+3)(n+2) - (2n+1)(n+3) = 3$.
\item \textbf{(Dichotomy).} Combining the two, infinite log-concavity of the total $d$-Hoggatt numbers holds at $d=1$ and fails already at $d=2$.
\item \textbf{(Structural reframing).} We explain the dichotomy as a summation-amplification effect and formulate conjectures extending strict log-convexity to all $d \ge 2$, together with a renormalization program under which infinite log-concavity is recovered.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}
Throughout, sequences are real-valued functions $a : \NN \to \RR$.

\begin{definition}[Log-concavity operator]
The \emph{log-concavity operator} $\Lop$ sends a sequence $a$ to the sequence
\[
(\Lop a)(n) = a_{n+1}^2 - a_n\, a_{n+2}, \qquad n \ge 0.
\]
The value $(\Lop a)(n)$ is the \emph{discriminant} of $a$ at index $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Log-concave / log-convex]
A sequence $a$ is \emph{log-concave} if $(\Lop a)(n) \ge 0$ for all $n$, and \emph{log-convex} if $(\Lop a)(n) \le 0$ for all $n$. It is \emph{strictly log-convex} if $(\Lop a)(n) < 0$ for all $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Infinite log-concavity]
A sequence $a$ is \emph{infinitely log-concave} if the $k$-fold iterate $\Lop^k a$ is log-concave for every $k \ge 0$; that is, $(\Lop^k a)(n) \ge 0$ for all $k, n \ge 0$ (with $\Lop^0 a = a$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Real Catalan numbers]
The Catalan numbers are defined by the ratio recurrence
\[
C_0 = 1, \qquad C_{n+1} = \frac{2(2n+1)}{n+2}\, C_n \quad (n \ge 0).
\]
This is equivalent to $C_n = \frac{1}{n+1}\binom{2n}{n}$ and generates $1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42, 132, 429, \dots$
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total $d$-Hoggatt numbers]
For the $d$-Hoggatt triangle $H_d(n,k)$, the \emph{total} is $H_d(n) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} H_d(n,k)$. We use $H_1(n) = 2^n$, $H_2(n) = C_n$, and $H_3(n) = B_n$ (Baxter numbers).
\end{definition}

\section{The $d=1$ case: log-linear totals}

\begin{lemma}[Annihilation of geometric sequences]
For every $r \in \RR$, the sequence $a_n = r^n$ satisfies $(\Lop a)(n) = 0$ for all $n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(\Lop a)(n) = (r^{n+1})^2 - r^n\cdot r^{n+2} = r^{2n+2} - r^{2n+2} = 0.$
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Persistence of the zero sequence]
If $\mathbf{0}$ denotes the identically-zero sequence, then $\Lop\mathbf{0} = \mathbf{0}$, hence $\Lop^k \mathbf{0} = \mathbf{0}$ for all $k$; the zero sequence is infinitely log-concave.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(\Lop\mathbf{0})(n) = 0^2 - 0\cdot 0 = 0$. Induction on $k$ gives $\Lop^k\mathbf{0} = \mathbf{0}$, and $0 \ge 0$ establishes log-concavity of every iterate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite log-concavity of geometric sequences]
For every $r \in \RR$, the sequence $a_n = r^n$ is infinitely log-concave. In particular, the total $1$-Hoggatt numbers $H_1(n) = 2^n$ are infinitely log-concave.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the annihilation lemma, $\Lop a = \mathbf{0}$. Thus $\Lop^0 a = a$ is log-concave (discriminant identically $0$), and for $k \ge 1$, $\Lop^k a = \Lop^{k-1}(\Lop a) = \Lop^{k-1}\mathbf{0} = \mathbf{0}$, log-concave by the persistence lemma. Take $r = 2$.
\end{proof}

\section{The $d=2$ case: strict log-convexity of the Catalan numbers}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity]
$C_n > 0$ for all $n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction: $C_0 = 1 > 0$, and if $C_n > 0$ then $C_{n+1} = \frac{2(2n+1)}{n+2}C_n$ is a product of positive factors.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The key identity]
For all real $n$, $(2n+3)(n+2) - (2n+1)(n+3) = 3.$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(2n+3)(n+2) = 2n^2 + 7n + 6$ and $(2n+1)(n+3) = 2n^2 + 7n + 3$; subtract.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Negative discriminant]
For all $n \ge 0$, $(\Lop C)(n) < 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Applying the ratio recurrence twice,
\[
C_{n+1} = \frac{2(2n+1)}{n+2}\,C_n, \qquad C_{n+2} = \frac{4(2n+1)(2n+3)}{(n+2)(n+3)}\,C_n.
\]
Hence
\[
(\Lop C)(n) = C_{n+1}^2 - C_n C_{n+2}
= \frac{4(2n+1)}{n+2}C_n^2\left[\frac{2n+1}{n+2} - \frac{2n+3}{n+3}\right].
\]
The bracket equals $\dfrac{(2n+1)(n+3) - (2n+3)(n+2)}{(n+2)(n+3)} = \dfrac{-3}{(n+2)(n+3)} < 0$ by the key identity. Therefore $(\Lop C)(n) = -\dfrac{12(2n+1)}{(n+2)^2(n+3)}\,C_n^2 < 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Catalan log-convexity]
The Catalan numbers are strictly log-convex: $C_{n+1}^2 < C_n C_{n+2}$ for all $n \ge 0$. Consequently they are not log-concave, and therefore not infinitely log-concave.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strict log-convexity is $(\Lop C)(n) < 0$ for all $n$. Log-concavity would require $(\Lop C)(0) \ge 0$, contradicted by $C_1^2 = 1 < 2 = C_0 C_2$. Failing log-concavity, it cannot be infinitely log-concave.
\end{proof}

\section{Main result: the dichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[Log-concavity dichotomy at the base of the Hoggatt hierarchy]
The total $1$-Hoggatt numbers $H_1(n) = 2^n$ are infinitely log-concave, while the total $2$-Hoggatt numbers $H_2(n) = C_n$ (the Catalan numbers) are not even log-concave. Infinite log-concavity holds at $d=1$ and fails at $d=2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first clause is the infinite log-concavity theorem for geometric sequences (with $r=2$); the second is Catalan log-convexity.
\end{proof}

The dichotomy refutes the guess embedded in the name of the phenomenon: log-concavity of the totals is not inherited from the (log-concave) rows; it is an exceptional feature of the single geometric member $d=1$.

\section{The $d=3$ case and the general mechanism}

\subsection{Baxter numbers}
The Baxter numbers admit the summation formula
\[
B_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{\binom{n+1}{k-1}\binom{n+1}{k}\binom{n+1}{k+1}}{\binom{n+1}{1}\binom{n+1}{2}},
\]
generating $1, 1, 2, 6, 22, 92, 422, 2074, \dots$ Direct computation gives $(\Lop B)(n) < 0$ at every computed index (e.g.\ $B_1^2 - B_0 B_2 = -1$; $B_2^2 - B_1 B_3 = -2$; $B_3^2 - B_2 B_4 = -8$), so the Baxter totals are empirically strictly log-convex, mirroring the Catalan case.

\subsection{Why summation amplifies growth}
Each total $H_d(n)$ is a positively weighted sum of products of binomial coefficients whose dominant contribution grows like $\rho_d^{\,n}\, n^{-\alpha_d}$ with $\rho_d > 1$, $\alpha_d > 0$ (Catalan: $\rho_2=4,\alpha_2=3/2$; Baxter: $\rho_3=8,\alpha_3=4$). For $a_n \sim \rho^n n^{-\alpha}$ with $\alpha > 0$, the ratios $a_{n+1}/a_n = \rho\,(1-\alpha/n+O(n^{-2}))$ increase in $n$---the signature of log-convexity. Summation superimposes terms of differing growth rates; the fastest wins increasingly, dragging the total into log-convexity. The $d=1$ case escapes because $\alpha_1 = 0$: no correction, no competing rates, nothing to amplify.

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Discriminant.} From $a_0,\dots,a_{N+1}$ output $(\Lop a)(n) = a_{n+1}^2 - a_n a_{n+2}$ for $0 \le n \le N-1$. Cost $O(N)$.
\item \textbf{Iterated test.} To test infinite log-concavity to depth $K$: apply the discriminant map $K$ times, each pass shrinking the window by two; report the first $(k,n)$ with a negative value. Cost $O(KN)$.
\item \textbf{Exact totals.} Generate Catalan numbers by the ratio recurrence and Baxter numbers by the triple-binomial sum, in exact rational arithmetic, so sign tests are rigorous.
\end{itemize}

\section{Applications and Discussion}
Log-concavity versus log-convexity dictates the qualitative shape of a sequence: log-concave sequences are unimodal; log-convex sequences accelerate monotonically. The dichotomy is a cautionary tale about inheritance: log-concave rows can produce strictly log-convex totals, so summation is not neutral with respect to log-behavior. For the \emph{totals}, the infinite log-concavity question has a negative, strong answer already at index $0$, redirecting attention to the rows and to renormalized totals.

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Universal log-convexity of the totals for $d \ge 2$]
For every $d \ge 2$, the total $d$-Hoggatt numbers are strictly log-convex: $H_d(n+1)^2 < H_d(n)H_d(n+2)$ for all $n \ge 0$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Log-convexity as a summation phenomenon]
If $T(n,k)$ is any triangle whose rows are log-concave and whose row-ratio $T(n+1,k)/T(n,k)$ is nondecreasing in $n$, then the row sums $S(n) = \sum_k T(n,k)$ are log-convex.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Infinite log-concavity of the rows]
For every $d \ge 1$, each fixed row $k \mapsto H_d(n,k)$ is infinitely log-concave; a candidate mechanism is a closure lemma with the golden-ratio-squared safety margin $a_k^2 \ge \frac{3+\sqrt5}{2}\,a_{k-1}a_{k+1}$, whose constant is the fixed point of the operator's worst-case ratio.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Log-concave renormalization of the totals]
For every $d \ge 2$ there is an explicit positive normalizing sequence $w_d(n)$ (e.g.\ a ratio of factorials) such that $H_d(n)/w_d(n)$ is infinitely log-concave, even though the raw totals are log-convex.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}
We proved a clean dichotomy at the base of the $d$-Hoggatt hierarchy: the $d=1$ totals $2^n$ are infinitely log-concave because $\Lop$ annihilates every geometric sequence, whereas the $d=2$ totals---the Catalan numbers---are strictly log-convex, the failure driven by the exact identity $(2n+3)(n+2)-(2n+1)(n+3)=3$. Numerical evidence extends strict log-convexity to the Baxter totals and points to a universal summation-amplification mechanism for $d \ge 2$. The main lesson is a reframing: for the totals, the natural object of study is log-\emph{convexity}, with infinite log-concavity surviving (conjecturally) only for the rows or a suitable renormalization.

